<h2 id="LifPhiWor">1. Life and philosophical works</h2>

<p>
Berkeley was born in 1685 near Kilkenny, Ireland. After several years
of schooling at Kilkenny College, he entered Trinity College, in
Dublin, at age 15. He was made a fellow of Trinity College in 1707
(three years after graduating) and was ordained in the Anglican Church
shortly thereafter. At Trinity, where the curriculum was notably
modern, Berkeley encountered the new science and philosophy of the
late seventeenth century, which was characterized by hostility towards
Aristotelianism. Berkeley&rsquo;s philosophical notebooks (sometimes
styled the <em>Philosophical Commentaries</em>), which he began in
1707, provide rich documentation of Berkeley&rsquo;s early
philosophical evolution, enabling the reader to track the emergence of
his immaterialist philosophy from a critical response to Descartes,
Locke, Malebranche, Newton, Hobbes, and others.</p>

<p>
Berkeley&rsquo;s first important published work, <em>An Essay Towards
a New Theory of Vision</em> (1709), was an influential contribution to
the psychology of vision and also developed doctrines relevant to his
idealist project. In his mid-twenties, he published his most enduring
works, the <em>Treatise concerning the Principles of Human
Knowledge</em> (1710) and the <em>Three Dialogues between Hylas and
Philonous</em> (1713), whose central doctrines we will examine below.
In this same early period, he also published <em>Passive
Obedience</em> (1712) which contains his most detailed discussion of
the foundations of morality. For further discussion of <em>Passive
Obedience</em> and Berkeley&rsquo;s ethical theory see 
 <a href="activism.html#B1PasObe">Supplement B.1</a>.</p>

<p>
In 1720, while completing a four-year tour of Europe as tutor to a
young man, Berkeley composed <em>De Motu</em>, a tract on the
philosophical foundations of mechanics which developed his views on
philosophy of science and articulated an instrumentalist approach to
Newtonian dynamics. After his continental tour, Berkeley returned to
Ireland and resumed his position at Trinity until 1724, when he was
appointed Dean of Derry. At this time, Berkeley began developing his
scheme for founding a college in Bermuda. He was convinced that Europe
was in spiritual decay and that the New World offered hope for a new
golden age. Having secured a charter and promises of funding from the
British Parliament, Berkeley set sail for America in 1728, with his
new bride, Anne Forster. They spent three years in Newport, Rhode
Island, awaiting the promised money, but Berkeley&rsquo;s political
support had collapsed and they were forced to abandon the project and
return to Britain in 1731. While in America, Berkeley composed
<em>Alciphron</em>, a work of Christian apologetics directed against
the &ldquo;free-thinkers&rdquo; whom he took to be enemies of
established Anglicanism. <em>Alciphron</em> is also a significant
philosophical work and a crucial source of Berkeley&rsquo;s views on
language. For more on Berkeley&rsquo;s views on language see 
 <a href="meaning.html">Supplement A</a>.</p>

<p>
Shortly after returning to London, Berkeley composed the <em>Theory of
Vision, Vindicated and Explained</em>, a defense of his earlier work
on vision, and <em>The Analyst</em>, an acute and influential critique
of the foundations of Newton&rsquo;s calculus. In 1734 he was made
Bishop of Cloyne, and thus he returned to Ireland. Shortly after
taking up his bishopric, Berkeley published <em>The Querist</em>, an
odd work consisting entirely of a numbered list of questions, in which
he addresses a variety of economic and social issues confronting
Ireland. Most notably, <em>The Querist</em>, in spite of its
interrogative form, lays out Berkeley&rsquo;s proposal for
establishing an Irish national bank, that was to issue and supervise a
system of paper currency for Ireland. Although Berkeley used in his
seat in the Irish parliament to lobby for his proposal it never gained
any significant backing. Further discussion of Berkeley&rsquo;s
economic thinking and the social theorizing contained in <em>The
Querist</em> can be found in Supplement 
 <a href="activism.html#B2BerEco">B.2</a> 
 and 
 <a href="activism.html#B3BerWelOrdSoc">B.3</a>.

It was also in Cloyne, that Berkeley wrote
his last, strangest, and best-selling (in his own lifetime)
philosophical work. <em>Siris</em> (1744) has a three-fold aim: to
establish the virtues of tar-water (a liquid prepared by letting pine
tar stand in water) as a medical panacea, to provide scientific
background supporting the efficacy of tar-water, and to lead the mind
of the reader, via gradual steps, toward contemplation of God.
Berkeley died in 1753, shortly after moving to Oxford to supervise the
education of his son George, one of the three out of seven of his
children to survive childhood.</p>

<h2 id="BerCriMatPriDia">2. Berkeley&rsquo;s critique of materialism in the <em>Principles</em> and <em>Dialogues</em></h2>

<p>
In his two great works of metaphysics, Berkeley defends idealism by
attacking the materialist alternative. What exactly is the doctrine
that he&rsquo;s attacking? Readers should first note that
&ldquo;materialism&rdquo; is here used to mean &ldquo;the doctrine
that material things exist&rdquo;. This is in contrast with another
use, more standard in contemporary discussions, according to which
materialism is the doctrine that <em>only</em> material things exist.
Berkeley contends that <em>no</em> material things exist, not just
that some immaterial things exist. Thus, he attacks Cartesian and
Lockean <em>dualism</em>, not just the considerably less popular (in
Berkeley&rsquo;s time) view, held by Hobbes, that only material things
exist. But what exactly is a material thing? Interestingly, part of
Berkeley&rsquo;s attack on matter is to argue that this question
cannot be satisfactorily answered by the materialists, that they
cannot characterize their supposed material things. However, an answer
that captures what exactly it is that Berkeley rejects is that
material things are <em>mind-independent</em> things or substances.
And a mind-independent thing is something whose existence is not
dependent on thinking/perceiving things, and thus would exist whether
or not any thinking things (minds) existed. Berkeley holds that there
are no such mind-independent things; this is expressed in the famous
phrase, <em>esse est percipi</em> (<em>aut percipere</em>)&mdash;to be
is to be perceived (or to perceive), though Berkeley himself used the
anglicized Latin <em>esse is percipi</em>.</p>

<p>
Berkeley charges that materialism promotes skepticism and atheism:
skepticism because materialism implies that our senses mislead us as
to the natures of these material things, which moreover need not exist
at all, and atheism because a material world could be expected to run
without the assistance of God. This double charge provides
Berkeley&rsquo;s <em>motivation</em> for questioning materialism (one
which he thinks should motivate others as well), though not, of
course, a philosophical argument against materialism. Fortunately, the
<em>Principles</em> and <em>Dialogues</em> overflow with such
arguments. Below, we will examine some of the main elements of
Berkeley&rsquo;s argumentative campaign against matter.</p>

<h3 id="AttRepMat">2.1 The attack on representationalist materialism</h3>

<h4>2.1.1 The core argument</h4>

<p>
The starting point of Berkeley&rsquo;s attack on the materialism of
his contemporaries is a very short argument presented in
<em>Principles</em> 4:</p>

<blockquote>
It is indeed an opinion strangely prevailing amongst men, that houses,
mountains, rivers, and in a word all sensible objects have an
existence natural or real, distinct from their being perceived by the
understanding. But with how great an assurance and acquiescence soever
this principle may be entertained in the world; yet whoever shall find
in his heart to call it in question, may, if we mistake not, perceive
it to involve a manifest contradiction. For what are the forementioned
objects but the things we perceive by sense, and what do we perceive
besides our own ideas or sensations; and is it not plainly repugnant
that any one of these or any combination of them should exist
unperceived?
</blockquote>

<p>
Berkeley presents here the following argument (see Winkler 1989,
138):</p>

<ol>

<li>We perceive ordinary objects (houses, mountains, etc.).</li>

<li>We perceive only ideas.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Therefore,</p>

<ol start="3">

<li>Ordinary objects are ideas.</li>
</ol>

<p>
The argument is valid, and premise (1) looks hard to deny. What about
premise (2)? Berkeley believes that this premise is accepted by all
the modern philosophers. In the <em>Principles</em>, Berkeley is
operating <em>within</em> the idea-theoretic tradition of the
seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. In particular, Berkeley believes
that some version of this premise is accepted by his main targets, the
influential philosophers Descartes and Locke.</p>

<p>
However, Berkeley recognizes that these philosophers have an obvious
response available to this argument. This response blocks
Berkeley&rsquo;s inference to (3) by distinguishing two sorts of
perception, mediate and immediate. Thus, premises (1) and (2) are
replaced by the claims that (1&prime;) we mediately perceive ordinary
objects, while (2&prime;) we immediately perceive only ideas. From
these claims, of course, no idealist conclusion follows. The response
reflects a <em>representationalist theory of perception</em>,
according to which we indirectly (mediately) perceive material things,
by directly (immediately) perceiving ideas, which are mind-dependent
items. The ideas <em>represent</em> external material objects, and
thereby allow us to perceive them.</p>

<p>
Whether Descartes, Malebranche, and Locke were representationalists of
this kind is a matter of some controversy (see e.g. Yolton 1984,
Chappell 1994). However, Berkeley surely had good grounds for
understanding his predecessors in this way: it reflects the most
obvious interpretation of Locke&rsquo;s account of perception and
Descartes&rsquo; whole procedure in the <em>Meditations</em> tends to
suggest this sort of view, given the meditator&rsquo;s situation as
someone contemplating her own ideas, trying to determine whether
something external corresponds to them.</p>

<h4>2.1.2 The likeness principle</h4>

<p>
Berkeley devotes the succeeding sections of the <em>Principles</em> to
undermining the representationalist response to his initial argument.
In effect, he poses the question: What allows an idea to represent a
material object? He assumes, again with good grounds, that the
representationalist answer is going to involve
<em>resemblance</em>:</p>

<blockquote>
But say you, though the ideas themselves do not exist without the
mind, yet there may be things like them whereof they are copies or
resemblances, which things exist without the mind, in an unthinking
substance. We answer, an idea can be like nothing but an idea; a
colour or figure can be like nothing but another colour or figure.
(PHK 8)
</blockquote>

<p>
Berkeley argues that this supposed resemblance is nonsensical; an idea
can only be like another idea.</p>

<p>
But why? The closest Berkeley ever comes to directly addressing this
question is in his early philosophical notebooks, where he observes
that &ldquo;Two things cannot be said to be alike or unlike till they
have been compar&rsquo;d&rdquo; (PC 377). Thus, because the mind can
compare nothing but its own ideas, which by hypothesis are the only
things immediately perceivable, the representationalist cannot assert
a likeness between an idea and a non-ideal mind-independent material
object. (For further discussion, see Winkler 1989, 145&ndash;9.)</p>

<p>
If Berkeley&rsquo;s Likeness Principle, the thesis that an idea can
only be like another idea, is granted, representationalist materialism
is in serious trouble. For how are material objects now to be
characterized? If material objects are supposed to be extended, solid,
or colored, Berkeley will counter that these sensory qualities pertain
to ideas, to that which is immediately perceived, and that the
materialist cannot assert that material objects are like ideas in
these ways. Many passages in the <em>Principles</em> and
<em>Dialogues</em> drive home this point, arguing that matter is, if
not an incoherent notion, at best a completely <em>empty</em> one.</p>

<h4>2.1.3 Anti-abstractionism</h4>

<p>
One way in which Berkeley&rsquo;s anti-abstractionism comes into play
is in reinforcing this point. Berkeley argues in the
&ldquo;Introduction&rdquo; to the
 <em>Principles</em><sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-1" id="ref-1">1</a>]</sup>
 that we cannot form general ideas in the way that Locke often seems
to suggest&mdash;by stripping particularizing qualities from an idea
of a particular, creating a new, intrinsically general,
<em>abstract</em>
 idea.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-2" id="ref-2">2</a>]</sup>
 Berkeley then claims that notions the materialist might invoke in a
last-ditch attempt to characterize matter, e.g. <em>being</em> or mere
extension, are objectionably abstract and
 unavailable.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-3" id="ref-3">3</a>]</sup>
 For more discussion of the &ldquo;Introduction&rdquo; to the
<em>Principles</em>, see
 <a href="meaning.html#IntPri">Supplement A.2</a>.</p>


<h4>2.1.4 What does materialism explain?</h4>

<p>
Berkeley is aware that the materialist has one important card left to
play: Don&rsquo;t we need material objects in order to
<em>explain</em> our ideas? And indeed, this seems intuitively
gripping: Surely the best explanation of the fact that I have a chair
idea every time I enter my office and that my colleague has a chair
idea when <em>she</em> enters my office is that a single enduring
material object <em>causes</em> all these various ideas. Again,
however, Berkeley replies by effectively exploiting the weaknesses of
his opponents&rsquo; theories:</p>

<blockquote>
&hellip;though we give the materialists their external bodies, they by
their own confession are never the nearer knowing how our ideas are
produced: since they own themselves unable to comprehend in what
manner body can act upon spirit, or how it is possible it should
imprint any idea in the mind. Hence it is evident the production of
ideas or sensations in our minds, can be no reason why we should
suppose matter or corporeal substances, since that is acknowledged to
remain equally inexplicable with, or without this supposition. (PHK
19)
</blockquote>

<p>
Firstly, Berkeley contends, a representationalist must admit that we
<em>could</em> have our ideas without there being any external objects
causing them (PHK 18). (This is one way in which Berkeley sees
materialism as leading to skepticism.) More devastatingly, however, he
must admit that the existence of matter does not help to explain the
occurrence of our ideas. After all, Locke himself diagnosed the
difficulty:</p>

<blockquote>
Body as far as we can conceive being able only to strike and affect
body; and Motion, according to the utmost reach of our <em>Ideas</em>,
being able to produce nothing but Motion, so that when we allow it to
produce pleasure or pain, or the <em>Idea</em> of a Colour, or Sound,
we are fain to quit our Reason, go beyond our <em>Ideas</em>, and
attribute it wholly to the good Pleasure of our Maker. (Locke
1689/1694 [1975], 541; <em>Essay</em> 4.3.6)
</blockquote>

<p>
And, when Descartes was pressed by Elizabeth as to how mind and body
 interact,<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-4" id="ref-4">4</a>]</sup>
 she rightly regarded his answers as unsatisfactory. The basic problem
here is set by dualism: how can one substance causally affect another
substance of a fundamentally different <em>kind</em>? In its Cartesian
form, the difficulty is particularly severe: how can an extended
thing, which affects other extended things only by mechanical impact,
affect a mind, which is non-extended and <em>non-spatial</em>?</p>

<p>
Berkeley&rsquo;s point is thus well taken. It is worth noting that, in
addition to undermining the materialist&rsquo;s attempted inference to
the best explanation, Berkeley&rsquo;s point also challenges any
attempt to explain representation and mediate perception in terms of
causation. That is, the materialist might try to claim that ideas
represent material objects, not by resemblance, but in virtue of being
<em>caused</em> by the objects. (Though neither Descartes nor Locke
spells out such an account, there are grounds in each for attributing
such an account to them. For Descartes see Wilson 1999, 73&ndash;76;
for Locke see Chappell 1994, 53.) However, PHK 19 implies that the
materialists are not in a position to render this account of
representation philosophically satisfactory.</p>

<h3 id="ConDirReaMat">2.2 Contra direct realist materialism</h3>

<p>
As emphasized above, Berkeley&rsquo;s campaign against matter, as he
presents it in the <em>Principles</em>, is directed against
materialist representationalism and presupposes representationalism.
In particular, Berkeley presupposes that all anyone ever directly or
immediately perceives are ideas. As contemporary philosophers, we
might wonder whether Berkeley has anything to say to a materialist who
denies this representationalist premise and asserts instead that we
ordinarily directly/immediately perceive material objects themselves.
The answer is &lsquo;yes&rsquo;.</p>

<h4>2.2.1 The master argument?</h4>

<p>
However, one place where one might naturally look for such an argument
is not, in fact, as promising as might initially appear. In both the
<em>Principles</em> (22&ndash;3) and the <em>Dialogues</em> (200),
Berkeley gives a version of what has come to be called &ldquo;The
Master
 Argument&rdquo;<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-5" id="ref-5">5</a>]</sup>
 because of the apparent strength with which he endorses it:</p>

<blockquote>
&hellip; I am content to put the whole upon this issue; if you can but
conceive it possible for one extended moveable substance, or in
general, for any one idea or any thing like an idea, to exist
otherwise than in a mind perceiving it, I shall readily give up the
cause&hellip;. But say you, surely there is nothing easier than to
imagine trees, for instance, in a park, or books existing in a closet,
and no body by to perceive them. I answer, you may so, there is no
difficulty in it: but what is all this, I beseech you, more than
framing in your mind certain ideas which you call <em>books</em> and
<em>trees</em>, and at the same time omitting to frame the idea of any
one that may perceive them? But do not you your self perceive or think
of them all the while? This therefore is nothing to the purpose: it
only shows you have the power of imagining or forming ideas in your
mind; but it doth not shew that you can conceive it possible, the
objects of your thought may exist without the mind: to make out this,
it is necessary that you conceive them existing unconceived or
unthought of, which is a manifest repugnancy. When we do our utmost to
conceive the existence of external bodies, we are all the while only
contemplating our own ideas. But the mind taking no notice of itself,
is deluded to think it can and doth conceive bodies existing unthought
of or without the mind; though at the same time they are apprehended
by or exist in it self. (PHK 22&ndash;23)
</blockquote>

<p>
The argument seems intended to establish that we cannot actually
conceive of mind-independent objects, that is, objects existing
unperceived and unthought of. Why not? Simply because in order to
conceive of any such things, we must ourselves be conceiving, i.e.,
thinking, of them. However, as Pitcher (1977, 113) nicely observes,
such an argument seems to conflate the <em>representation</em> (what
we conceive <em>with</em>) and the <em>represented</em> (what we
conceive <em>of</em>&mdash;the content of our thought). Once we make
this distinction, we realize that although we must have some
conception or representation in order to conceive of something, and
<em>that representation</em> is in some sense thought of, it does not
follow (contra Berkeley) that what we conceive <em>of</em> must be a
thought-of object. That is, when we imagine a tree standing alone in a
forest, we (arguably) conceive of an unthought-of object, though of
course we must employ a thought in order to accomplish this
 feat.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-6" id="ref-6">6</a>]</sup>
 Thus (as many commentators have observed), <em>this</em> argument
fails.</p>

<p>
A more charitable reading of the argument (see Winkler 1989,
184&ndash;7; Lennon 1988) makes Berkeley&rsquo;s point that we cannot
represent unconceivedness, because we have never and could never
experience
 it.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-7" id="ref-7">7</a>]</sup>
 Because we cannot represent unconceivedness, we cannot conceive of
mind-independent objects. While this is a rather more promising
argument, it clearly presupposes representationalism, just as
Berkeley&rsquo;s earlier <em>Principles</em> arguments
 did.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-8" id="ref-8">8</a>]</sup>
 (This, however, is not necessarily a defect of the interpretation,
since the <em>Principles</em>, as we saw above, is aimed against
representationalism, and in the <em>Dialogues</em> the Master Argument
crops up only after Hylas has been converted to representationalism
(see
 below).)<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-9" id="ref-9">9</a>]</sup></p>
 
<h4>2.2.2 The First Dialogue and relativity arguments</h4>

<p>
Thus, if we seek a challenge to direct realist materialism, we must
turn to the <em>Three Dialogues</em>, where the character Hylas (the
would-be materialist) begins from a sort of na&iuml;ve realism,
according to which we perceive material objects themselves, directly.
Against this position, Philonous (lover of
spirit&mdash;Berkeley&rsquo;s spokesperson) attempts to argue that the
sensible qualities&mdash;the qualities immediately perceived by
sense&mdash;must be ideal, rather than belonging to material objects.
(The following analysis of these first dialogue arguments is indebted
to Margaret Wilson&rsquo;s account in &ldquo;Berkeley on the
Mind-Dependence of Colors,&rdquo; Wilson 1999,
 229&ndash;242.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-10" id="ref-10">10</a>]</sup>)</p>
 
<p>
Philonous begins his first argument by contending that sensible
qualities such as heat are not distinct from pleasure or pain.
Pleasure and pain, Philonous argues, are allowed by all to be merely
in the mind; therefore the same must be true for the sensible
qualities. The most serious difficulties with this argument are (1)
whether we should grant the &ldquo;no distinction&rdquo; premise in
the case of the particular sensory qualities invoked by Berkeley (why
not suppose that wecan distinguish between the heat and the pain?) and
(2) if we do, whether we should generalize to all sensory qualities as
Berkeley would have us do.</p>

<p>
Secondly, Philonous invokes relativity arguments to suggest that
because sensory qualities are relative to the perceiver, e.g. what is
hot to one hand may be cold to the other and what is sweet to one
person may be bitter to another, they cannot belong to
mind-independent material objects, for such objects could not bear
contradictory qualities.</p>

<p>
As Berkeley is well aware, one may reply to this sort of argument by
claiming that only one of the incompatible qualities is truly a
quality of the object and that the other apparent qualities result
from <em>misperception</em>. But how then, Berkeley asks, are these
&ldquo;true&rdquo; qualities to be identified and distinguished from
the &ldquo;false&rdquo; ones (3D 184)? By noting the differences
between animal perception and human perception, Berkeley suggests that
it would be arbitrary anthropocentrism to claim that humans have
special access to the true qualities of objects. Further, Berkeley
uses the example of microscopes to undermine the <em>prima facie</em>
plausible thought that the true visual qualities of objects are
revealed by close examination. Thus, Berkeley provides a strong
challenge to any direct realist attempt to specify standard conditions
under which the true (mind-independent) qualities of objects are
(directly) perceived by sense.</p>

<p>
Under this pressure from Philonous, Hylas retreats (perhaps a bit
quickly) from na&iuml;ve realism to a more &ldquo;philosophical&rdquo;
position. He first tries to make use of the primary/secondary quality
distinction associated with mechanism and, again, locatable in the
thought of Descartes and Locke. Thus, Hylas allows that color, taste,
etc. may be mind-dependent (secondary) qualities, but contends that
figure, solidity, motion and rest (the primary qualities) exist in
mind-independent material bodies. The mechanist picture behind this
proposal is that bodies are composed of particles with size, shape,
motion/rest, and perhaps solidity, and that our sensory ideas arise
from the action of such particles on our sense organs and, ultimately,
on our minds. Berkeley opposes this sort of mechanism throughout his
writings, believing that it engenders skepticism by dictating that
bodies are utterly unlike our sensory experience of them. Here
Philonous has a two-pronged reply: (1) The same sorts of relativity
arguments that were made against secondary qualities can be made
against primary ones. (2) We cannot abstract the primary qualities
(e.g. shape) from secondary ones (e.g. color), and thus we cannot
conceive of mechanist material bodies which are extended but not (in
themselves)
 colored.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-11" id="ref-11">11</a>]</sup></p>
 
<p>
When, after some further struggles, Hylas finally capitulates to
Philonous&rsquo; view that all of existence is mind-dependent, he does
so unhappily and with great reluctance. Philonous needs to convince
him (as Berkeley needed to convince his readers in both books) that a
commonsensical philosophy could be built on an immaterialist
foundation, that no one but a skeptic or atheist would ever miss
matter. As a matter of historical fact, Berkeley persuaded few of his
contemporaries, who for the most part regarded him as a purveyor of
skeptical paradoxes (Bracken 1965). Nevertheless, we can and should
appreciate the way in which Berkeley articulated a positive idealist
philosophical system, which, if not in perfect accord with common
sense, is in many respects superior to its competitors.</p>

<h2 id="BerPosProIdeComSen">3. Berkeley&rsquo;s positive program: idealism and common sense</h2>

<h3 id="BasBerOnt">3.1 The basics of Berkeley&rsquo;s ontology</h3>

<h4>3.1.1 The status of ordinary objects</h4>

<p>
The basics of Berkeley&rsquo;s metaphysics are apparent from the first
section of the main body of the <em>Principles</em>:</p>

<blockquote>
It is evident to any one who takes a survey of the objects of human
knowledge, that they are either ideas actually imprinted on the
senses, or else such as are perceived by attending to the passions and
operations of the mind, or lastly ideas formed by help of memory and
imagination, either compounding, dividing, or barely representing
those originally perceived in the aforesaid ways. By sight I have the
ideas of light and colours with their several degrees and variations.
By touch I perceive, for example, hard and soft, heat and cold, motion
and resistance, and of all these more and less either as to quantity
or degree. Smelling furnishes me with odours; the palate with tastes,
and hearing conveys sounds to the mind in all their variety of tone
and composition. And as several of these are observed to accompany
each other, they come to be marked by one name, and so to be reputed
as one thing. Thus, for example, a certain colour, taste, smell,
figure and consistence having been observed to go together, are
accounted one distinct thing, signified by the name apple. Other
collections of ideas constitute a stone, a tree, a book, and the like
sensible things; which, as they are pleasing or disagreeable, excite
the passions of love, hatred, joy, grief, and so forth.
</blockquote>

<p>
As this passage illustrates, Berkeley does not deny the existence of
ordinary objects such as stones, trees, books, and apples. On the
contrary, as was indicated above, he holds that only an immaterialist
account of such objects can avoid skepticism about their existence and
nature. What such objects turn out to be, on his account, are bundles
or collections of ideas. An apple is a combination of visual ideas
(including the sensible qualities of color and visual shape), tangible
ideas, ideas of taste, smell,
 etc.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-12" id="ref-12">12</a>]</sup>
 The question of what does the combining is a philosophically
interesting one which Berkeley does not address in detail. He does
make clear that there are two sides to the process of bundling ideas
into objects: (1) co-occurrence, an objective fact about what sorts of
ideas tend to accompany each other in our experience, and (2)
something we do when we decide to single out a set of co-occurring
ideas and refer to it with a certain name (NTV 109).</p>

<p>
Thus, although there is no material world for Berkeley, there is a
physical world, a world of ordinary objects. This world is
mind-dependent, for it is composed of ideas, whose existence consists
in being perceived. For ideas, and so for the physical world, <em>esse
est percipi</em>.</p>

<h4>3.1.2 Spirits as active substances</h4>

<p>
Berkeley&rsquo;s ontology is not exhausted by the ideal, however. In
addition to perceived things (ideas), he posits perceivers, i.e.,
minds or <em>spirits</em>, as he often terms them. Spirits, he
emphasizes, are totally different in kind from ideas, for they are
active where ideas are passive. This suggests that Berkeley has
replaced one kind of dualism, of mind and matter, with another kind of
dualism, of mind and idea. There is something to this point, given
Berkeley&rsquo;s refusal to elaborate upon the relation between active
minds and passive ideas. At <em>Principles</em> 49, he famously
dismisses quibbling about <em>how</em> ideas inhere in the mind (are
minds colored and extended when such sensible qualities &ldquo;exist
in&rdquo; them?) with the declaration that &ldquo;those qualities are
in the mind only as they are perceived by it, that is, not by way of
<em>mode</em> or <em>attribute</em>, but only by way of
<em>idea</em>&rdquo;. Berkeley&rsquo;s dualism, however, is a dualism
<em>within</em> the realm of the mind-dependent.</p>

<h4>3.1.3 God&rsquo;s existence</h4>

<p>
The last major item in Berkeley&rsquo;s ontology is God, himself a
spirit, but an infinite one. Berkeley believes that once he has
established idealism, he has a novel and convincing argument for
God&rsquo;s existence as the cause of our sensory ideas. He argues by
elimination: What could cause my sensory ideas? Candidate causes,
supposing that Berkeley has already established that matter
doesn&rsquo;t exist, are (1) other ideas, (2) myself, or (3) some
other spirit. Berkeley eliminates the first option with the following
argument (PHK 25):</p>

<ol>

<li>Ideas are manifestly passive&mdash;no power or activity is
perceived in them.</li>

<li>But because of the mind-dependent status of ideas, they cannot
have any characteristics which they are not perceived to have.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Therefore,</p>

<ol start="3">

<li>Ideas are passive, that is, they possess no causal power.</li>
</ol>

<p>
It should be noted that premise (2) is rather strong; Phillip Cummins
(1990) identifies it as Berkeley&rsquo;s &ldquo;manifest qualities
thesis&rdquo; and argues that it commits Berkeley to the view that
ideas are radically and completely dependent on perceivers in the way
that sensations of pleasure and pain are typically taken to
 be.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-13" id="ref-13">13</a>]</sup></p>
 
<p>
The second option is eliminated with the observation that although we
clearly can cause some ideas at will (e.g. ideas of imagination),
sensory ideas are involuntary; they present themselves whether we wish
to perceive them or not and we cannot control their content. The
hidden assumption here is that any causing the mind does must be done
by willing and such willing must be accessible to consciousness.
Berkeley is hardly alone in presupposing this model of the mental;
Descartes, for example, makes a similar set of assumptions.</p>

<p>
This leaves us, then, with the third option: my sensory ideas must be
caused by some other spirit. Berkeley thinks that when we consider the
stunning complexity and systematicity of our sensory ideas, we must
conclude that the spirit in question is wise and benevolent beyond
measure, that, in short, he is God.</p>

<h3 id="RepObj">3.2 Replies to objections</h3>

<p>
With the basic ingredients of Berkeley&rsquo;s ontology in place, we
can begin to consider how his system works by seeing how he responds
to a number of intuitively compelling objections to it. Berkeley
himself sees very well how necessary this is: Much of the
<em>Principles</em> is structured as a series of objections and
replies, and in the <em>Three Dialogues</em>, once Philonous has
rendered Hylas a reluctant convert to idealism, he devotes the rest of
the book to convincing him that this is a philosophy which coheres
well with common sense, at least better than materialism ever did.</p>

<h4>3.2.1 Real things vs. imaginary ones</h4>

<p>
Perhaps the most obvious objection to idealism is that it makes real
things no different from imaginary ones&mdash;both seem fleeting
figments of our own minds, rather than the solid objects of the
materialists. Berkeley replies that the distinction between real
things and chimeras retains its full force on his view. One way of
making the distinction is suggested by his argument for the existence
of God, examined above: Ideas which depend on our own finite human
wills are not (constituents of) real things. Not being voluntary is
thus a necessary condition for being a real thing, but it is clearly
not sufficient, since hallucinations and dreams do not depend on our
wills, but are nevertheless not real. Berkeley notes that the ideas
that constitute real things exhibit a steadiness, vivacity, and
distinctness that chimerical ideas do not. The most crucial feature
that he points to, however, is <em>order</em>. The ideas imprinted by
the author of nature as part of <em>rerum</em> natura occur in regular
patterns, according to the laws of nature (&ldquo;the set rules or
established methods, wherein the mind we depend on excites in us the
ideas of sense, are called the Laws of Nature&rdquo; PHK 30). They are
thus regular and coherent, that is, they constitute a coherent real
world.</p>

<h4>3.2.2 Hidden structures and internal mechanisms</h4>

<p>
The related notions of regularity and of the laws of nature are
central to the workability of Berkeley&rsquo;s idealism. They allow
him to respond to the following objection, put forward in PHK 60:</p>

<blockquote>
&hellip;it will be demanded to what purpose serves that curious
organization of plants, and the admirable mechanism in the parts of
animals; might not vegetables grow, and shoot forth leaves and
blossoms, and animals perform all their motions, as well without as
with all that variety of internal parts so elegantly contrived and put
together, which being ideas have nothing powerful or operative in
them, nor have any necessary connexion with the effects ascribed to
them? [&hellip;] And how comes it to pass, that whenever there is any
fault in the going of a watch, there is some corresponding disorder to
be found in the movements, which being mended by a skilful hand, all
is right again? The like may be said of all the clockwork of Nature,
great part whereof is so wonderfully fine and subtle, as scarce to be
discerned by the best microscope. In short, it will be asked, how upon
our principles any tolerable account can be given, or any final cause
assigned of an innumerable multitude of bodies and machines framed
with the most exquisite art, which in the common philosophy have very
apposite uses assigned them, and serve to explain abundance of
phenomena.
</blockquote>

<p>
Berkeley&rsquo;s answer, for which he is indebted to
 Malebranche,<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-14" id="ref-14">14</a>]</sup>
 is that, although God could make a watch run (that is, produce in us
ideas of a watch running) without the watch having any internal
mechanism (that is, without it being the case that, were we to open
the watch, we would have ideas of an internal mechanism), he cannot do
so if he is to act in accordance with the laws of nature, which he has
established for our benefit, to make the world regular and
predictable. Thus, whenever we have ideas of a working watch, we will
find that if we open
 it,<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-15" id="ref-15">15</a>]</sup>
 we will see (have ideas of) an appropriate internal mechanism.
Likewise, when we have ideas of a living tulip, we will find that if
we pull it apart, we will observe the usual internal structure of such
plants, with the same transport tissues, reproductive parts, etc.</p>

<h4>3.2.3 Scientific explanation</h4>

<p>
Implicit in the answer above is Berkeley&rsquo;s insightful account of
scientific explanation and the aims of science. A bit of background is
needed here to see why this issue posed a special challenge for
Berkeley. One traditional understanding of science, derived from
Aristotle, held that it aims at identifying the causes of things.
Modern natural philosophers such as Descartes narrowed science&rsquo;s
domain to <em>efficient</em> causes and thus held that science should
reveal the efficient causes of natural things, processes, and
 events.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-16" id="ref-16">16</a>]</sup>
 Berkeley considers this as the source of an objection at
<em>Principles</em> 51:</p>

<blockquote>
Seventhly, it will upon this be demanded whether it does not seem
absurd to take away natural causes, and ascribe every thing to the
immediate operation of spirits? We must no longer say upon these
principles that fire heats, or water cools, but that a spirit heats,
and so forth. Would not a man be deservedly laughed at, who should
talk after this manner? I answer, he would so; in such things we ought
to <em>think with the learned, and speak with the vulgar</em>.
</blockquote>

<p>
On Berkeley&rsquo;s account, the true cause of any phenomenon is a
spirit, and most often it is the same spirit, namely, God.</p>

<p>
But surely, one might object, it is a step backwards to abandon our
scientific theories and simply note that God causes what happens in
the physical world! Berkeley&rsquo;s first response here, that we
should think with the learned but speak with the vulgar, advises us to
continue to say that fire heats, that the heart pumps blood, etc. What
makes this advice legitimate is that he can reconstrue such talk as
being about regularities in our ideas. In Berkeley&rsquo;s view, the
point of scientific inquiry is to reveal such regularities:</p>

<blockquote>
If therefore we consider the difference there is betwixt natural
philosophers and other men, with regard to their knowledge of the
<em>phenomena</em>, we shall find it consists, not in an exacter
knowledge of the efficient cause that produces them, for that can be
no other than the <em>will of a spirit</em>, but only in a greater
largeness of comprehension, whereby analogies, harmonies, and
agreements are discovered in the works of Nature, and the particular
effects explained, that is, reduced to general rules, see
<em>Sect.</em> 62, which rules grounded on the analogy, and
uniformness observed in the production of natural effects, are most
agreeable, and sought after by the mind; for that they extend our
prospect beyond what is present, and near to us, and enable us to make
very probable conjectures, touching things that may have happened at
very great distances of time and place, as well as to predict things
to come&hellip;. (PHK 105)
</blockquote>

<p>
Natural philosophers thus consider signs, rather than causes (PHK
108), but their results are just as useful as they would be under a
materialist system. Moreover, the regularities they discover provide
the sort of explanation proper to science, by rendering the particular
events they subsume unsurprising (PHK 104). The sort of explanation
proper to science, then, is not causal explanation, but reduction to
 regularity.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-17" id="ref-17">17</a>]</sup></p>
 
<h4>3.2.4 Unperceived objects&mdash;<em>Principles</em> vs. <em>Dialogues</em></h4>

<p>
Regularity provides a foundation for one of Berkeley&rsquo;s responses
to the objection summarized in the famous limerick:</p>

<blockquote>
There was a young man who said God,
<br />
must think it exceedingly odd
<br />
if he finds that the tree
<br />
continues to be
<br />
when no one&rsquo;s about in the
 Quad.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-18" id="ref-18">18</a>]</sup>
 </blockquote>

<p>
The worry, of course, is that if to be is to be perceived (for
non-spirits), then there are no trees in the Quad at 3 a.m. when no
one is there to perceive them and there is no furniture in my office
when I leave and close the door. Interestingly, in the
<em>Principles</em> Berkeley seems relatively unperturbed by this
natural objection to idealism. He claims that there is no problem
for</p>

<blockquote>
&hellip;anyone that shall attend to what is meant by the term
<em>exist</em> when applied to sensible things. The table I write on,
I say, exists, that is, I see and feel it; and if I were out of my
study I should say it existed, meaning thereby that if I was in my
study I might perceive it, or that some other spirit actually does
perceive it. (PHK 3)
</blockquote>

<p>
So, when I say that my desk still exists after I leave my office,
perhaps I just mean that I would perceive it if I were in my office,
or, more broadly, that a finite mind would perceive the desk were it
in the appropriate circumstances (in my office, with the lights on,
with eyes open, etc.). This is to provide a sort of counterfactual
analysis of the continued existence of unperceived objects. The truth
of the counterfactuals in question is anchored in regularity: because
God follows set patterns in the way he causes ideas, I would have a
desk idea if I were in the office.</p>

<p>
Unfortunately, this analysis has counterintuitive consequences when
coupled with the <em>esse est percipi</em> doctrine (McCracken 1979,
286). If to be is, as Berkeley insists, to be perceived, then the
unperceived desk does not exist, despite the fact that it
<em>would</em> be perceived and thus <em>would</em> exist if someone
opened the office door. Consequently, on this view the desk would not
endure uninterrupted but would pop in and out of existence, though it
would do so quite predictably. One way to respond to this worry would
be to dismiss it&mdash;what does it matter if the desk ceases to exist
when unperceived, as long as it exists whenever we need it? Berkeley
shows signs of this sort of attitude in <em>Principles</em>
45&ndash;46, where he tries to argue that his materialist opponents
and scholastic predecessors are in much the same
 boat.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-19" id="ref-19">19</a>]</sup>
 This &ldquo;who cares?&rdquo; response to the problem of continued
existence is fair enough as far as it goes, but it surely does
conflict with common sense, so if Berkeley were to take this route he
would have to moderate his claims about his system&rsquo;s ability to
accommodate everything desired by the person on the street.</p>

<p>
Another strategy, however, is suggested by Berkeley&rsquo;s reference
in PHK 3 and 48 to &ldquo;some other spirit,&rdquo; a strategy
summarized in a further limerick:</p>

<blockquote>
Dear Sir, your astonishment&rsquo;s odd
<br />
I am always about in the Quad
<br />
And that&rsquo;s why the tree
<br />
continues to be
<br />
since observed by, Yours faithfully, God
</blockquote>

<p>
If the other spirit in question is God, an omnipresent being, then
perhaps his perception can be used to guarantee a completely
continuous existence to every physical object. In the <em>Three
Dialogues</em>, Berkeley very clearly invokes God in this context.
Interestingly, whereas in the <em>Principles</em>, as we have seen
above, he argued that God must exist in order to <em>cause</em> our
ideas of sense, in the Dialogues (212, 214&ndash;5) he argues that our
ideas must <em>exist in</em> God when not perceived by
 us.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-20" id="ref-20">20</a>]</sup>
 If our ideas exist in God, then they presumably exist continuously.
Indeed, they must exist continuously, since standard Christian
doctrine dictates that God is unchanging.</p>

<p>
Although this solves one problem for Berkeley, it creates several
more. The first is that Berkeley&rsquo;s other commitments, religious
and philosophical, dictate that God cannot literally have our ideas.
Our ideas are sensory ideas and God is a being who &ldquo;can suffer
nothing, nor be affected with any painful sensation, or indeed any
sensation at all&rdquo; (3D 206). Nor can our sensory ideas be copies
of God&rsquo;s nonsensory ones (McCracken 1979):</p>

<blockquote>
How can that which is sensible be like that which is insensible? Can a
real thing in itself <em>invisible</em> be like a <em>colour</em>; or
a real thing which is not <em>audible</em>, be like a <em>sound</em>?
(3D 206)
</blockquote>

<p>
A second problem is that God&rsquo;s ideas are eternal, whereas
physical objects typically have finite duration. And, even worse, God
has ideas of all possible objects (Pitcher 1977, 171&ndash;2), not
just the ones which we would commonsensically wish to say exist.</p>

<p>
A solution (proposed by McCracken) to these related problems is to tie
the continued existence of ordinary objects to God&rsquo;s will,
rather than to his understanding. McCracken&rsquo;s suggestion is that
unperceived objects continue to exist as God&rsquo;s decrees. Such an
account in terms of divine decrees or volitions looks promising: The
tree continues to exist when unperceived just in case God has an
appropriate volition or intention to cause a tree-idea in finite
perceivers under the right circumstances. Furthermore, this solution
has important textual support: In the <em>Three Dialogues</em>, Hylas
challenges Philonous to account for the creation, given that all
existence is mind-dependent, in his view, but everything must exist
eternally in the mind of God. Philonous responds as follows:</p>

<blockquote>
May we not understand it [the creation] to have been entirely in
respect of finite spirits; so that things, with regard to us, may
properly be said to begin their existence, or be created, when God
decreed they should become perceptible to intelligent creatures, in
that order and manner which he then established, and we now call the
laws of Nature? You may call this a <em>relative</em>, or
<em>hypothetical existence</em> if you please. (3D 253)
</blockquote>

<p>
Here Berkeley ties the actual existence of created physical beings to
God&rsquo;s decrees, that is, to his will.</p>

<p>
As with the counterfactual analysis of continued existence, however,
this account also fails under pressure from the <em>esse est
percipi</em> principle:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
Hylas. Yes, Philonous, I grant the existence of a sensible thing
consists in being perceivable, but not in being actually
perceived.</p>

<p>
Philonous. And what is perceivable but an idea? And can an idea exist
without being actually perceived? These are points long since agreed
between us. (3D 234)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Thus, if the only grounds of continued existence are
<em>volitions</em> in God&rsquo;s mind, rather than perceived items
(ideas), then ordinary objects do not exist continuously, but rather
pop in and out of existence in a lawful fashion.</p>

<p>
Fortunately, Kenneth Winkler has put forward an interpretation which
goes a great distance towards resolving this difficulty. In effect, he
proposes that we amend the &ldquo;volitional&rdquo; interpretation of
the existence of objects with the hypothesis that Berkeley held
&ldquo;the denial of blind agency&rdquo; (Winkler 1989,
207&ndash;224). This principle, which can be found in many authors of
the period (including Locke), dictates that any volition must have an
idea behind it, that is, must have a cognitive component that gives
content to the volition, which would otherwise be empty or
&ldquo;blind&rdquo;. While the principle is never explicitly invoked
or argued for by Berkeley, in a number of passages he does note the
interdependence of will and understanding. Winkler plausibly suggests
that Berkeley may have found this principle so obvious as to need no
arguing. With it in place, we have a guarantee that anything willed by
God, e.g. that finite perceivers in appropriate circumstances should
have elm tree ideas, also has a divine idea associated with it.
Furthermore, we have a neat explanation of Berkeley&rsquo;s
above-noted leap in the <em>Dialogues</em> from the claim that God
must cause our ideas to the claim that our ideas must exist in
God.</p>

<p>
Of course, it remains true that God cannot have ideas that are,
strictly speaking, the same as ours. This problem is closely related
to another that confronts Berkeley: Can two people ever perceive the
same thing? Common sense demands that two students can perceive the
same tree, but Berkeley&rsquo;s metaphysics seems to dictate that they
never truly perceive the same thing, since they each have their own
numerically distinct ideas. One way to dissolve this difficulty is to
recall that objects are bundles of ideas. Although two people cannot
perceive/have the numerically same idea, they can perceive the same
object, assuming that perceiving a component of the bundle suffices
for perception of the
 bundle.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-21" id="ref-21">21</a>]</sup>
 Another proposal (Baxter 1991) is to invoke Berkeley&rsquo;s doctrine
that &ldquo;same&rdquo; has both a philosophical and a vulgar sense
(3D 247) in order to declare that my tree-idea and your tree-idea are
strictly distinct but loosely (vulgarly) the same. Either account
might be applied in order to show either that God and I may perceive
the same object, or that God and I may perceive, loosely speaking, the
same thing.</p>

<p>
From this discussion we may draw a criterion for the actual existence
of ordinary objects, one which summarizes Berkeley&rsquo;s considered
views:</p>

<blockquote>
An <em>X</em> exists at time <em>t</em> if and only if God has an idea
that corresponds to a volition that if a finite mind at <em>t</em> is
in appropriate circumstances (e.g. in a particular place, looking in
the right direction, or looking through a microscope), then it will
have an idea that we would be disposed to call a perception of an
<em>X</em>.
</blockquote>

<p>
This captures the idea that existence depends on God&rsquo;s
perceptions, but only on the perceptions which correspond to or are
included in his volitions about what <em>we</em> should perceive. It
also captures the fact that the bundling of ideas into objects is done
by
 us.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-22" id="ref-22">22</a>]</sup></p>
 
<h4>3.2.5 The possibility of error</h4>

<p>
A further worry about Berkeley&rsquo;s system arises from the
idea-bundle account of
 objects.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-23" id="ref-23">23</a>]</sup>
 If there is no mind-independent object against which to measure my
ideas, but rather my ideas help to constitute the object, then how can
my ideas ever fail&mdash;how is error possible? Here is another way to
raise the worry that we have in mind: We saw above that
Berkeley&rsquo;s arguments against commonsense realism in the first
<em>Dialogue</em> attempt to undermine (1) claims that heat, odor,
taste are distinguishable from pleasure/pain and (2) the claim that
objects have one true color, one true shape, one true taste, etc. If
we then consider what this implies about Berkeleyian objects, we must
conclude that Berkeley&rsquo;s cherry is red, purple, gray, tart,
sweet, small, large, pleasant, and painful! It seems that
Berkeley&rsquo;s desire to refute the mechanist representationalism
which dictates that objects are utterly <em>unlike</em> our experience
of them has lead him to push <em>beyond</em> common sense to the view
that objects are <em>exactly like</em> our experience of
 them.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-24" id="ref-24">24</a>]</sup>
 There is no denying that Berkeley is out of sync with common sense
here. He does, however, have an account of error, as he shows us in
the <em>Dialogues</em>:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
Hylas. What say you to this? Since, according to you, men judge of the
reality of things by their senses, how can a man be mistaken in
thinking the moon a plain lucid surface, about a foot in diameter; or
a square tower, seen at a distance, round; or an oar, with one end in
the water, crooked?</p>

<p>
Philonous. He is not mistaken with regard to the ideas he actually
perceives; but in the inferences he makes from his present
perceptions. Thus in the case of the oar, what he immediately
perceives by sight is certainly crooked; and so far he is in the
right. But if he thence conclude, that upon taking the oar out of the
water he shall perceive the same crookedness; or that it would affect
his touch, as crooked things are wont to do: in that he is mistaken.
(3D 238)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
Extrapolating from this, we may say that my gray idea of the cherry,
formed in dim light, is not in itself wrong and forms a part of the
bundle-object just as much as your red idea, formed in daylight.
However, if I judge that the cherry would look gray in bright light,
I&rsquo;m in error. Furthermore, following Berkeley&rsquo;s directive
to speak with the vulgar, I ought not to say (in ordinary
circumstances) that &ldquo;the cherry is gray,&rdquo; since that will
be taken to imply that the cherry would look gray to humans in
daylight.</p>

<h4>3.2.6 Spirits and causation</h4>

<p>
We have spent some time examining the difficulties Berkeley faces in
the &ldquo;idea/ordinary object&rdquo; half of his ontology. Arguably,
however, less tractable difficulties confront him in the realm of
spirits. Early on, Berkeley attempts to forestall materialist skeptics
who object that we have no idea of spirit by arguing for this position
himself:</p>

<blockquote>
A spirit is one simple, undivided, active being: as it perceives
ideas, it is called the <em>understanding</em>, and as it produces or
otherwise operates about them, it is called the <em>will</em>. Hence
there can be no idea formed of a soul or spirit: for all ideas
whatever, being passive and inert, <em>vide Sect.</em> 25, they cannot
represent unto us, by way of image or likeness, that which acts. A
little attention will make it plain to any one, that to have an idea
which shall be like that active principle of motion and change of
ideas, is absolutely impossible. Such is the nature of <em>spirit</em>
or that which acts, that it cannot be of it self perceived, but only
by the effects which it produceth. (PHK 27)
</blockquote>

<p>
Surely the materialist will be tempted to complain, however, that
Berkeley&rsquo;s unperceivable spiritual substances, lurking behind
the scenes and supporting that which we can perceive, sound a lot like
the material substances which he so emphatically rejects.</p>

<p>
Two very different responses are available to Berkeley on this issue,
each of which he seems to have made at a different point in his
philosophical development. One response would be to reject spiritual
substance just as he rejected material substance. Spirits, then, might
be understood in a Humean way, as bundles of ideas and volitions.
Fascinatingly, something like this view is considered by Berkeley in
his early philosophical notebooks (see PC 577ff). Why he abandons it
is an interesting and difficult
 question;<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-25" id="ref-25">25</a>]</sup>
 it seems that one worry he has is how the understanding and the will
are to be integrated and rendered one thing.</p>

<p>
The second response would be to explain why spiritual substances are
better posits than material ones. To this end, Berkeley emphasizes
that we have a notion of spirit, which is just to say that we know
what the word means. This purportedly contrasts with
&ldquo;matter,&rdquo; which Berkeley thinks has no determinate
content. Of course, the real question is: How does the term
&ldquo;spirit&rdquo; come by any content, given that we have no idea
of it? In the <em>Principles</em>, Berkeley declares only that we know
spirit through our own case and that the content we assign to
&ldquo;spirit&rdquo; is derived from the content each of us assigns to
&ldquo;I&rdquo; (PHK 139&ndash;140). In the <em>Dialogues</em>,
however, Berkeley shows a better appreciation of the force of the
problem that confronts him:</p>

<blockquote>
[Hylas.] You say your own soul supplies you with some sort of an idea
or image of God. But at the same time you acknowledge you have,
properly speaking, no idea of your own soul. You even affirm that
spirits are a sort of beings altogether different from ideas.
Consequently that no idea can be like a spirit. We have therefore no
idea of any spirit. You admit nevertheless that there is spiritual
substance, although you have no idea of it; while you deny there can
be such a thing as material substance, because you have no notion or
idea of it. Is this fair dealing? To act consistently, you must either
admit matter or reject spirit. (3D 232)
</blockquote>

<p>
To the main point of Hylas&rsquo; attack, Philonous replies that each
of us has, in our own case, an immediate intuition of ourselves, that
is, we know our own minds through reflection (3D 231&ndash;233).
Berkeley&rsquo;s considered position, that we gain access to ourselves
as thinking things through conscious awareness, is surely an intuitive
one. Nevertheless, it is disappointing that he never gave an explicit
response to the Humean challenge he entertained in his notebooks:</p>

<blockquote>
+ Mind is a congeries of Perceptions. Take away Perceptions &amp; you
take away the Mind put the Perceptions &amp; you put the mind. (PC
580)
</blockquote>

<p>
A closely related problem which confronts Berkeley is how to make
sense of the causal powers that he ascribes to spirits. Here again,
the notebooks suggest a surprisingly Humean view:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
+ The simple idea call&rsquo;d Power seems obscure or rather none at
all. but onely the relation &rsquo;twixt cause &amp; Effect. Wn I ask
whether A can move B. if A be an intelligent thing. I mean no more
than whether the volition of A that B move be attended with the motion
of B, if A be senseless whether the impulse of A against B be
follow&rsquo;d by ye motion of B.
 461<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-26" id="ref-26">26</a>]</sup></p>
 
<p>
S What means Cause as distinguish&rsquo;d from Occasion? nothing but a
Being wch wills wn the Effect follows the volition. Those things that
happen from without we are not the Cause of therefore there is some
other Cause of them i.e., there is a being that wills these
perceptions in us. 499</p>

<p>
S There is a difference betwixt Power &amp; Volition. There may be
volition without Power. But there can be no Power without Volition.
Power implyeth volition &amp; at the same time a Connotation of the
Effects following the Volition. 699</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
461 suggests the Humean view that a cause is whatever is
 (regularly)<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-27" id="ref-27">27</a>]</sup>
 followed by an effect. 499 and 699 revise this doctrine by requiring
that a cause not only (regularly) precede an effect but also be a
volition. Berkeley&rsquo;s talk of occasion here reveals the immediate
influence of Malebranche. Malebranche held that the only true cause is
God and that apparent finite causes are only &ldquo;occasional
causes,&rdquo; which is to say that they provide occasions for God to
act on his general volitional policies. Occasional
&ldquo;causes&rdquo; thus regularly precede their
&ldquo;effects&rdquo; but are not truly responsible for producing
them. In these notebook entries, however, Berkeley seems to be
suggesting that all there is to causality is this regular consequence,
with the first item being a volition. Such an account, unlike
Malebranche&rsquo;s, would make my will and God&rsquo;s will causes in
exactly the same thin sense.</p>

<p>
Some commentators, most notably Winkler, suppose that Berkeley retains
this view of causality in the published works. The main difficulty
with this interpretation is that Berkeley more than once purports to
inspect our idea of body, and the sensory qualities included therein,
and to conclude from that inspection that bodies are passive (DM 22,
PHK 25). This procedure would make little sense if bodies, according
to Berkeley, fail to be causes by definition, simply because they are
not minds with
 wills.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-28" id="ref-28">28</a>]</sup>
 What is needed is an explanation of what Berkeley <em>means</em> by
activity, which he clearly equates with causal power. Winkler (1989,
130&ndash;1) supplies such an account, according to which activity
means direction towards an end. But this is to identify efficient
causation with final causation, a controversial move at best which
Berkeley would be making without comment or argument.</p>

<p>
The alternative would be to suppose, as <em>De Motu</em> 33 suggests,
that Berkeley holds that we gain a notion of activity, along with a
notion of spirit as substance, through reflective awareness/internal
consciousness:</p>

<blockquote>
[W]e feel it [mind] as a faculty of altering both our own state and
that of other things, and that is properly called vital, and puts a
wide distinction between soul and bodies. (DM 33)
</blockquote>

<p>
On this interpretation, Berkeley would again have abandoned the
radical Humean position entertained in his notebooks, as he clearly
did on the question of the nature of spirit. One can only speculate as
to whether his reasons would have been primarily philosophical,
theological, or practical. Berkeley&rsquo;s writings, however, are not
generally characterized by deference to authority, quite the
 contrary,<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-29" id="ref-29">29</a>]</sup>
 as he himself proclaims:</p>

<blockquote>
&hellip; one thing, I know, I am not guilty of. I do not pin my faith
on the sleeve of any great man. I act not out of prejudice &amp;
prepossession. I do not adhere to any opinion because it is an old
one, a receiv&rsquo;d one, a fashionable one, or one that I have spent
much time in the study and cultivation of. (PC 465)
</blockquote>

<h2 id="BerNatPhi">4. Berkeley&rsquo;s natural philosophy</h2>

<h3 id="BerVis">4.1 Berkeley on vision</h3>

<p>
Berkeley&rsquo;s <em>Essay towards a New Theory of Vision</em> [NTV],
first published in 1709, was the first of his major publications and
one of his most influential. Much of its content (and most of its
influence) is focused on issues that we would now regard as a part of
perceptual psychology or vision science rather than the metaphysics
and epistemology for which Berkeley is best known now. In it, Berkeley
puts forward a theory of the visual perception of spatial properties
(distance, size, place) and its central claim is that none of these
properties are immediately perceived by sight. Rather, the immediate
objects of vision serve as signs, learned from experience, for the
spatial properties as perceived by touch.</p>

<p>
The framework in which Berkeley conducts his discussion of vision is
that derived from Kepler and shared with other early modern writers,
most importantly Descartes and Malebranche. In 1604 Kepler published
his <em>Optics</em>, which revolutionized understanding of the optics
of the eye. In the <em>Optics</em>, Kepler argues that the cornea and
lens combine to focus light on the retina, forming an inverted (and
left-right reversed) image on the retina. The immediate stimulus for
vision is this image and all of the information available for visual
perception is to be found in the retinal image. The Keplerian picture
immediately raises questions about how spatial properties are visually
perceived. Distance from the perceiver is not represented in the
retinal image, the size of an object&rsquo;s projection on the retina
(visual angle) does not on its own specify the object&rsquo;s size,
and the retinal image is inverted and left-right reversed. Answering
these questions was the focus of early modern discussions of
 vision.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-30" id="ref-30">30</a>]</sup></p>
 
<h4>4.1.1 Berkeley on visual perception</h4>

<p>
The goals of the <em>New Theory of Vision</em>, as Berkeley tells us
at the beginning of the book, are to:</p>

<blockquote>
&hellip;shew the manner, wherein we perceive by sight the distance,
magnitude, and situation of objects. Also to consider the difference
there is betwixt the ideas of sight and touch, and whether there be
any idea common to both senses. (NTV 1)
</blockquote>

<p>
There are two connected parts to this goal. The first is the obvious
one of explaining his new theory, how it differs from the alternative
views, and arguing for its superiority. The second one is to show
that, in fact, spatial properties are never immediately perceived via
vision and that the proper objects of vision are distinct from those
of touch and have no properties in common. This second thesis is often
called the heterogeneity thesis. The relation between ideas of touch
and vision is one of correlation without any necessary connection or
overlap of features between the two. In what follows, we will give an
overview of how he treats visual perception of three important
categories of spatial properties: distance, size, and position.</p>

<p>
One important tool that Berkeley uses to both argue for and illustrate
his theory is a thought experiment usually attributed to William
Molyneux, an important Irish natural philosopher of the late 17th
century. The question Molyneux asks concerns the visual experience and
abilities of someone born blind who has their vision restored as an
adult. The specific question Molyneux asked is whether such a person
who is familiar with spheres and cubes from touch could tell which is
which using only their restored vision or whether they would need to
learn the connection between the tangible and visual perceptions of
the two shapes. Molyneux&rsquo;s problem was widely discussed,
including by Locke, and Berkeley turns to the question of the nature
of the visual experience of someone with restored vision repeatedly in
the <em>New Theory of Vision</em>. His answer, given that the ideas of
sight and touch have nothing common, is always that the person with
restored vision will have to learn the correlation between the ideas
of the two senses. He uses the example as a tool to explain and
clarify how his theory applies to a variety of cases. In every case,
he clearly thinks it is obviously true that nothing in the nature of
the visual experience itself would allow the person with restored
vision to know whether he is looking at a cube or a sphere. [Reference
to SEP Molyneux article]</p>

<h4>4.1.2 Distance</h4>

<p>
Berkeley focuses first on distance, and he begins his discussion of
distance by affirming the Keplerian picture:</p>

<blockquote>
It is, I think, agreed by all that distance of itself, and immediately
cannot be seen. For distance being a line directed end-wise to the
eye, it projects only one point in the fund of the eye. Which point
remains invariably the same, whether the distance be longer or
shorter. (NTV 2)
</blockquote>

<p>
Berkeley and his contemporaries agree that although distance is not
immediately visually perceived it is perceived by sight and the
challenge is to offer an account of exactly how vision gives rise to
perception of distance.</p>

<p>
One way to solve this problem is to appeal to geometric facts related
to vision and this is the approach taken by Descartes and Malebranche
among others. If both eyes fixate on an object at a moderate distance,
then the angle defined by the optic axes of the two eyes will specify
the distance of that object (given the distance between the eyes). The
closer to parallel the optic axes of the two eyes are, the further
away the object is. Visual perception of distance relies, according to
this proposal, on necessary, geometric truths. Berkeley rejects this
the account, and any other based on mathematical truths, because the
angles and lines to which they appeal are not themselves registered by
vision. According to Berkeley, &ldquo;It is evident that when the mind
perceives any idea, not immediately and of itself, it must be by the
means of some other idea.&rdquo; (NTV 9) Since we don&rsquo;t have any
ideas of the properties that figure in the geometrical theories, those
theories can&rsquo;t provide an explanation of the visual perception
of distance. Berkeley proposes instead that the way in which we
perceive distance by sight relies not on necessary mathematical truths
but rather on correlations between visual ideas and distance that are
learned from experience. For example, although Berkeley rejects the
claim that the angle between the optic axes is relevant to visual
perception of distance, he thinks that the muscular sensations we have
of the way in which our eyes are oriented with respect to each other
does play a role in distance perception. There is no necessary
connection between the ideas we have of the orientation of our eyes
and distance but there is a correlation between those ideas and
distance to be found within our experience. And that correlation
establishes a &ldquo;habitual or customary connexion between these two
sorts of ideas.&rdquo; (NTV 17) Berkeley discusses a number of other
ideas that are correlated with distance including faintness,
confusedness (blur), and intervening objects. These correlations give
rise to habitual connections in which the perception of one of these
ideas visually immediately suggests the appropriate idea of
distance.</p>

<p>
The question of what concept of distance Berkeley is working with is
one that has generated confusion among commentators (Atherton 1990,
73-77). As Atherton points out, much of Berkeley&rsquo;s discussion
only makes sense if distance is understood in terms of how far away
something is from the perceiver. This is the kind of distance that can
be measured by pacing it off or with a tape measure and it is what is
correlated with the muscular sensations from vergence and the other
visual ideas Berkeley discusses. Berkeley himself often appears to
conflate distance in this sense with distance in the sense of outside
the mind. In a discussion of Molyneux&rsquo;s Problem, he sums up his
earlier discussion saying: &ldquo;For our judging objects perceived by
sight to be at any distance, or without the mind, is &hellip; intirely
the effect of experience&hellip;&rdquo; (NTV 41). It&rsquo;s not at
all clear how to interpret &ldquo;without the mind&rdquo; in this
context, nor is it clear whether &ldquo;or&rdquo; signals a
disjunction or a clarification. This claim and others like it have
also helped give rise to a debate as to the relation between the
<em>New Theory of Vision</em> and the <em>Principles</em> (Hight
2006). Since it&rsquo;s clear that Berkeley&rsquo;s main focus in the
<em>New Theory of Vision</em> is on distance in the measurable sense,
that&rsquo;s what we will focus on here.</p>

<p>
Since Berkeley&rsquo;s view is that visual ideas suggest ideas of
distance, there must be a source of ideas of distance. And that source
is, according to Berkeley, touch. Touch here is to be understood
broadly and includes kinesthetic sensations (ideas concerning the
disposition of our body parts) as well as touch proper. Seeing a thing
at a distance is for it to be the case that:</p>

<blockquote>
Having of a long time experienced certain ideas, perceivable by touch,
as distance, tangible figure, and solidity, to have been connected
with certain ideas of sight, I do upon perceiving these ideas of sight
forthwith conclude what tangible ideas are, by the wonted ordinary
course of Nature like to follow. Looking at an object I perceive a
certain visible figure and colour, with some degree of faintness and
other circumstances, which from what I have formerly observed,
determine me to think that if I advance forward so many paces or
miles, I shall be affected with such and such ideas of touch&hellip;
(NTV 45)
</blockquote>

<p>
The role of vision in the process of perceiving distance is to provide
us with ideas that allow us to predict what tangible ideas we will
have conditional on our acting in a specific way. There is nothing in
the visual ideas themselves, other than their correlation with
tangible ideas, that connects them to distance. In fact, Berkeley
concludes, we neither see the same things that we touch nor is there
any property in common between visual ideas and tangible ideas.
Suggestion, based on experienced correlation, is the only relation
between our visual and tangible ideas.</p>

<h4>4.1.3 Magnitude and situation</h4>

<p>
Berkeley&rsquo;s accounts of the visual perception of size (magnitude)
and position (situation) are similar to his account of seeing
distance. In both cases, visual ideas come to suggest, as a result of
experienced correlation, tangible ideas. And, in both cases, he
rejects any attempt to rely on geometrical facts. This has interesting
consequences for his account of the perception of both size and
position. His accounts of how we see size and position also illustrate
his commitment to there being no properties in common between vision
and touch.</p>

<p>
There is an obvious geometric relation between the size of the retinal
image of an object, distance to the object, and the size of the
physical object that is the cause of that image. The farther away an
object is from the eye, the smaller the image of that object on the
retina. This relation has led to an obvious hypothesis about how it is
that we are able to see, with reasonable accuracy, the physical size
of objects. We take the retinal image size and combine it with a
distance estimate to get a physical size estimate. Theories of this
kind dominated discussions of size perception in Berkeley&rsquo;s
time, continue to dominate discussion today, but are explicitly
rejected by Berkeley (NTV 53). Instead, Berkeley proposes that the
same correlations between visual and tangible ideas that allow us to
visually perceive distance also allow us to visually perceive size.
Rather than starting with a visual size estimate that is then adjusted
on the basis of a distance estimate, the various cues to distance are
also cues for size. There is visual size, but it is a different
property from physical or tangible size and, because of this, the
geometrical relationship relied on by others doesn&rsquo;t apply. The
visual size of an object is directly correlated with tangible size via
visual cues such as faintness and blur, but distance is not
 involved.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-31" id="ref-31">31</a>]</sup></p>
 
<p>
In the case of position, Berkeley&rsquo;s focus is on the apparent
problems caused by the fact that the retinal image is inverted and
left-right reversed. Again, Berkeley rejects any attempt to appeal to
geometry (or geometrical optics) in explaining why the world is
visually perceived as right-side up. According to Berkeley, position
in the visual field has no intrinsic relation to position in the
physical (tangible) world. Position in the visual field, however, is
correlated with tangible ideas of position. Although the retinal image
has a position with respect to tangible space, the visual field itself
has no such position. And that entails that the visual field is
neither inverted (or right side up) nor reversed (or not reversed)
with respect to tangible space. The relation between visible position
and tangible position results from experienced correlation between the
two and the inversion of the retinal image causes no problem.</p>

<h4>4.1.4 Visual Language</h4>

<p>
What we immediately perceive visually is not itself of any importance
to us according to Berkeley. We care about things, &ldquo;&hellip;in
proportion as they are adapted to benefit or injure our own bodies,
and thereby produce in our minds the sensations of pleasure and
pain.&rdquo; (NTV 59) Visual ideas are not connected to bodily
well-being and bodily pain and pleasure in the way that tangible ideas
are. So the value to us of vision is the ability it gives us to
predict what tangible ideas we will have if we perform a certain
action:</p>

<blockquote>
[B]y the perception of visible ideas (which in themselves are not
capable of affecting or any wise altering the frame of their bodies)
they may be able to foresee (from the experience they have had what
tangible ideas are connected with such and such visible ideas) and
damage or benefit which is like to ensue, upon the application of
their own bodies to this or that body which is at a distance. (NTV 59)
</blockquote>

<p>
Because our visual ideas of space don&rsquo;t have significance in
themselves, Berkeley thinks we typically disregard them and focus
instead on the tangible ideas they suggest. He draws an analogy with
language and the way in which, for fluent speakers, we focus on the
content and not the specific words used to communicate that content
(NTV 51). The lack of any necessary connection between our visual
ideas and the tangible ideas with which they are correlated allows
Berkeley to take the analogy a step further. The connection between
words and what they stand for is arbitrary in the sense that it
results from a convention that could have been otherwise. Similarly,
the correlations between visual ideas and tangible ideas could have
been different. In this case, it&rsquo;s not a human convention that
establishes the correlation but rather God&rsquo;s causing our visual
ideas in a way that correlates them with tangible ideas. From this
Berkeley draws the conclusion:</p>

<blockquote>
I think we may fairly conclude that the proper objects of vision
constitute an universal language of the Author of Nature, whereby we
are instructed how to regulate our actions in order to attain those
things that are necessary to the preservation and well-being of our
bodies, as also to avoid whatever may be hurtful and destructive of
them. (NTV 147)
</blockquote>

<p>
Berkeley was so taken with this aspect of his theory that when he
published a defense and summary of the central doctrines of the theory
of vision in 1733, he titled it, &ldquo;The Theory of Vision or Visual
Language shewing the immediate presence and Providence of a Deity
Vindicated and Explained&rdquo;.</p>

<h4>4.1.5 Visual space</h4>

<p>
One important issue in understanding the structure of Berkeley&rsquo;s
theory has received relatively little attention until fairly recently.
Berkely is quite clear both that objects of vision and touch are
distinct and that there are no shared properties between the immediate
objects of vision and the immediate objects of touch. He also argues
that the immediate objects of vision do not lie on a two-dimensional
plane. (NTV 157&ndash;58) But all that we are clearly told of what we
immediately perceive visually is that it includes light and color. In
particular, we are not told clearly whether the light and color that
we immediately perceive are organized in any kind of structural way
prior to our learning how they are correlated with tangible
perception. There are two possibilities here. One is that there is no
structure to our visual ideas prior to their coordination with
tangible ideas. Vision, on its own, presents us with a chaotic jumble
of ideas of light and color and the relationships that we are aware of
between different colored areas are based on their coordination with
the three-dimensional space of touch. The other possibility is that
even prior to coordination with touch the immediate ideas of vision
are organized in a space-like way. Although our ordinary talk of
situation and spatial relations is drawn from touch there is a
space-like organization that vision has on its own. There is a
two-dimensional manifold in which the lights and colors that we
immediately perceive are located and it has sufficient structure so
that there are facts about the size of areas of color in visual space
and separation in visual space.</p>

<p>
It&rsquo;s tempting to attribute to Berkeley a commitment to the first
possibility. His insistence on the heterogeneity of vision and touch,
that the objects of vision do not lie on a 2-D plane, and his denial
that geometry applies to vision on its own (NTV 154&ndash;56) all
point in this direction. On the other hand, he doesn&rsquo;t clearly
make this statement and his argument at NTV 156 that geometry
can&rsquo;t be applied to visual space is based on our visual ideas
being variable and inconstant, and not on lacking space-like
structure. It&rsquo;s also hard to see how we could learn the
correlations between visual ideas and tangible distance if there were
no structure in which colors and lights are visually presented. In
those correlations that Berkeley discusses in more detail, like the
perception of the angle of the eyes and faintness of the visual ideas
being correlated there is structure to the muscular sensations and the
varying degrees of faintness that will allow for meaningful
correlation. The two most careful discussions of this issue, by Rick
Grush and Robert Schwartz, agree that Berkeley&rsquo;s theory requires
a structured visual space but disagree as to whether Berkeley clearly
realized this or not (Grush 2007, Schwartz 2019)</p>

<h4>4.1.6 Action-experience connections</h4>

<p>
Berkeley often writes as if particular visual ideas are correlated
with particular tangible ideas and that once we learn these
correlations the visual ideas will suggest the corresponding tangible
ideas. And commentors on Berkeley sometimes follow him in describing
his view in this way. But this can&rsquo;t be a correct description of
the relation between our visual and tangible ideas because the
required correlations are rarely, if ever, to be found in our
experience. The correlations that we do find in our experience are all
contingent on how we act. I can see on my desk that there is a can of
pop to my left and volume 1 of Luce and Jessop to my right. If I reach
to my left, I will have tangible experiences of the cold, smooth can.
If I move my hand to my right, I will have tangible experiences of the
rougher, warmer cover, and the edge where the cheap binding tape
begins. Our visual experience does serve to guide action but rather
than directly predicting tangible experiences it informs us of the
tangible consequences of the various actions we might perform (Hilbert
and Perry 1994, pp. x&ndash;xi). Berkely seems to have appreciated
this point and explicitly describes this kind of connection in places.
In NTV 45 (quoted above) he describes how the visual idea of a faint
object predicts the tangible experiences we will have if we walk a
specific distance in that direction. The universal language of the
author of nature doesn&rsquo;t predict tangible ideas. Instead, it
informs us what we need to do to get the tangible ideas we want and
avoid the ones we don&rsquo;t.</p>

<h3 id="DeMot">4.2 <em>De Motu</em></h3>

<p>
Berkeley&rsquo;s first biographer, Joseph Stock, tells us that
Berkeley composed <em>De Motu</em> as a submission to a prize essay
competition conducted by the Paris Academy of Sciences. The only
available further evidence for this claim about the origins of <em>De
Motu</em> is circumstantial, but convincing&mdash;the essay was
written in Latin, it treats the proposed topic, and it includes
favorable and sympathetic references to Descartes and Cartesianism,
while not directly expressing any version of idealism. It seems also
clear, however, that in <em>De Motu</em> Berkeley puts forward views
motivated by the systematic philosophy of the <em>Principles</em> and
<em>Dialogues</em>. Indeed, more strongly, he is (among other things)
solving problems for his idealist system, posed or apparently posed by
the physics of his time.</p>

<p>
Like the <em>Principles</em>, <em>De Motu</em> begins by warning us
against being misled by language. His central concern is a set of
words that include: <em>solicitation of gravity</em>, <em>urge</em>,
<em>dead forces</em>, <em>effort</em>, <em>conation</em>,
<em>gravity</em>, <em>force</em>, and <em>attraction</em>. The worry
is that such words, in the writings of natural philosophers such as
Galileo, Torricelli, Leibniz, and Newton, may mislead the unwary into
supposing that the bodies treated by physics have causal powers. Thus,
what worries Berkeley is not primarily motion (or kinematics), which
is sensible and thus in principle amenable to an idealist analysis,
but rather dynamic notions, which seem causal. Berkeley is well aware
that dynamic theories cannot simply be dismissed, for in his works
from early to late, he acknowledges the extraordinary success of
Newton&rsquo;s <em>Principia Mathematica</em> (e.g. PHK 110, S 243).
But for Berkeley to fully integrate Newton&rsquo;s physics requires
more tools than he provides in the <em>Principles</em> and
<em>Dialogues</em>.</p>

<p>
Towards this end, Berkeley makes (at least) three relevant moves in
<em>De Motu</em>: (1) He assembles a variety of arguments and appeals
to authority to reinforce the conclusion that the physical world is
passive, and activity should be assigned only to minds and God. (2) He
outlines an anti-realist understanding of dynamics, which allows him
to endorse the Newton of the <em>Principia</em>. (3) He expands his
understanding of &ldquo;law of nature&rdquo; and scientific
explanation in a way that can accommodate Newton&rsquo;s mechanics and
allow that it provides scientific explanation. In what follows, we
will elaborate briefly on all three.</p>

<h4>4.2.1 The passivity of the physical world</h4>

<p>
As for (1), it is tempting to think that Berkeley overplays his hand,
since Berkeley&rsquo;s quasi-occasionalist version of
 passivity<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-32" id="ref-32">32</a>]</sup>
 is surely more radical than can plausibly be portrayed as standard or
uncontroversial in the period. Nevertheless, he is of course correct
to point out there were widespread concerns about how to reconcile
Newtonian attractionism with a conception of matter as passive backed
both by mechanist physics and a religiously motivated contrast between
passive matter and active spirit/mind/soul. Further, the question of
what sort of physical forces could legitimately be attributed to
matter itself was in fact a debated one. But Berkeley&rsquo;s most
philosophical move here is to argue, in effect, that we can&rsquo;t
take dynamics literally because force terms don&rsquo;t refer (DM
23&ndash;4, 29).</p>

<h4>4.2.2 Anti-realism about dynamics</h4>

<p>
Instead, dynamic terms must be understood as &ldquo;mathematical
hypotheses&rdquo;:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
DM 17. &lsquo;Force&rsquo;, &lsquo;gravity&rsquo; (gravitas),
&lsquo;attraction&rsquo;, and terms of this kind are useful for
rational argumentation and the calculation of motion and bodies in
motion, but not for the plain understanding of the nature of motion
itself, or for referring to a number of separate qualities. Certainly,
as regards attraction, Newton referred to it, not as a real and
physical quality, but only as a mathematical hypothesis.
[&hellip;]</p>

<p>
DM 18. The case is similar to the composition and resolution of direct
forces into oblique ones by the diagonal and sides of a parallelogram.
These things are helpful in mechanics and computation, but it is one
thing to serve in calculation and mathematical demonstration, quite
another to describe the nature of things.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
While there has been debate about what attitude exactly Berkeley
should be understood as advocating towards the dynamic theories that
include mathematical hypotheses, it seems clear that he is warning the
reader against a straightforwardly realistic interpretation of these
theories. We will be misled if we suppose that dynamic terms denote
physical qualities or describe the nature of things. Instead, we
should realize that their justification lies in their utility for
accurate predictions and calculations regarding the motions of bodies.
This suggests that Berkeley in effect is recommending and adopting an
instrumentalist attitude towards dynamics. One alternative
interpretive suggestion is that Berkeley is a reductionist rather than
an instrumentalist about dynamics, which is to say that he thinks talk
of forces (dynamics) can and should be reduced to talk of motions
(kinematics). There is some prima facie textual evidence for this
interpretation, though it is easy enough to explain away: Berkeley
holds that the reality behind dynamics, which Newtonian theory allow
us to better predict, is bodies in motion. However, Berkeley neither
offers nor is in a position to offer any systematic method for
translation between force-talk and motion-talk. Rather, the
justification for endorsing the theory is utility, as instrumentalism
has
 it.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-33" id="ref-33">33</a>]</sup>
 (Some further points about how to understand dynamics
appear in Alciphron; see 
 <a href="meaning.html#ForGra">Supplement A.3.3</a>.)</p>

<h4>4.2.3 Laws of nature and scientific explanation</h4>

<p>
Recall (from 3.2.3 above) that in the <em>Principles</em> and
<em>Dialogues</em>, Berkeley considers the question of how to
understand the goal and use of natural philosophy, given that (as he
argues) that goal is not to discover the (efficient) causes of the
 phenomena.<sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-34" id="ref-34">34</a>]</sup>
 His answer is that natural philosophy seeks to discover the
regularities in the phenomena that allow us to predict and retrodict;
that is, natural philosophy seeks to reduce the phenomena to general
rules (PHK 105). This picture poses at least a prima facie problem for
accommodating Newton&rsquo;s dynamics. The difficulty is that
Newton&rsquo;s laws, or any principles including dynamic terms, do not
individually describe regularities in the phenomena, and so
don&rsquo;t clearly qualify as laws of nature for Berkeley.</p>

<p>
In <em>De Motu</em>, Berkeley elaborates and expands his correlated
conceptions of law of nature and scientific explanation in a way that
allows him to give Newton&rsquo;s physics the status he clearly thinks
it has: our best example of successful theorizing in natural
philosophy, and one which provides scientific explanation through its
system of laws. Berkeley develops an expanded sense of &ldquo;law of
nature&rdquo; according to which the laws of nature are the most
general principles from which observed regularities in the phenomena
can be deduced:</p>

<blockquote>

<p>
Similarly in mechanical philosophy those are to be called principles,
in which the whole discipline is grounded and contained, those primary
laws of motions which have been proved by experiments, elaborated by
reason and rendered universal. These laws of motion are conveniently
called principles, since from them are derived both general mechanical
theorems and particular explanations of the phenomena.</p>

<p>
A thing can be said to be explained mechanically then indeed when it
is reduced to those most simple and universal principles, and shown by
accurate reasoning to be in agreement and connection with them. (DM
35&ndash;36)</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
This is an account of laws of nature and explanation that suits
Newton&rsquo;s <em>Principia</em>, and seems clearly intended to do
so.
 <sup>[<a href="notes.html#note-35" id="ref-35">35</a>]</sup></p>
 
<p>
It should be noted, that while the understanding of laws of nature and
scientific explanation that Berkeley articulates in <em>De Motu</em>
is quite general, that is, he applies it to natural philosophy
generally, the instrumentalist attitude that he articulates is not.
Rather, instrumentalism is an appropriate attitude to adopt towards a
theory, like Newtonian dynamics, which cannot be understood
realistically (because it includes non-referring terms that function
as mathematical hypotheses) but which is consistently useful in
allowing us to deduce regularities in the phenomena. It would thus be
a mistake, or at least over-hasty, to view Berkeley as an
instrumentalist generally about the science of his time.</p>

<h3 id="Sir">4.3 Siris</h3>

<p>
That Berkeley is not an instrumentalist generally about the science of
his time is nicely illustrated by <em>Siris</em>, Berkeley&rsquo;s
last major work (1744). <em>Siris</em> is undeniably a rather strange
book. It could hardly be otherwise, given Berkeley&rsquo;s aims in
writing it, which seem to be three-fold: &ldquo;to communicate to the
public the salutary virtues of tar-water,&rdquo; to provide scientific
background supporting the efficacy of tar-water as a medicine, and to
lead the mind of the reader, via gradual steps, toward contemplation
of God. The latter two aims shape Berkeley&rsquo;s use of contemporary
science in <em>Siris</em>. In particular, Berkeley&rsquo;s focus on
fire (i.e. aether or light) as quasi-universal &ldquo;cause&rdquo; of
natural change serves these purposes, for the &ldquo;activity&rdquo;
of the aether, in his view, can both explain the miraculous virtues of
a certain medicine and reveal God&rsquo;s action and his divine order.
Berkeley&rsquo;s corpuscular speculations, including his use of
fire-theory, were not idiosyncratic, as natural philosophy. In his
theorizing, as Jessop and other have noted, he is heavily indebted to
the work of Hermann Boerhaave, the Dutch chemist, botanist, and
physician whose teachings, especially as expressed in his <em>Elementa
Chemiae</em>, were highly influential in mid-eighteenth century
Britain.</p>

<p>
Berkeley makes heavy use of dynamic concepts in his theorizing in
<em>Siris</em>. He voices a general preference for Newtonian
force-based explanations of physical phenomena over strictly
mechanical Cartesian theories:</p>

<blockquote>
Nature seems better known and explained by attractions and repulsions
than by those other mechanical principles of size, figure, and the
like; that is, by Sir Isaac Newton than Descartes. (S 243)
</blockquote>

<p>
However, Berkeley makes clear that the dynamic elements of his
theorizing are not to be understood realistically, not as literal
attributions of real forces to particles; to say that particles
attract/are attracted is just to say that their movements agree with
certain laws:</p>

<blockquote>
The laws of attraction and repulsion are to be regarded as laws of
motion; and these only as rules or methods observed in the productions
of natural effects, the efficient and final causes whereof are not of
mechanical consideration. (S 231)
</blockquote>

<p>
Indeed, Berkeley explicitly echoes his <em>De Motu</em> interpretation
of forces as mathematical hypotheses, useful fictions:</p>

<blockquote>
There appears a uniform working in things great and small, by
attracting and repelling forces. But the particular laws of attraction
and repulsion are various. Nor are we concerned at all about the
forces, neither can we know or measure them otherwise than by their
effects, that is to say, the motions; which motions only, and not the
forces, are indeed in the bodies (Sect. 155). Bodies are moved to or
from each other, and this is performed according to different laws.
The natural or mechanic philosopher endeavours to discover those laws
by experiment and reasoning. But what is said of forces residing in
bodies, whether attracting or repelling, is to be regarded only as a
mathematical hypothesis, and not as anything really existing in
nature. (S 234)
</blockquote>

<p>
By contrast, Berkeley&rsquo;s attitude towards corpuscles in
<em>Siris</em> is consistently realistic, in the sense that all the
sorts of particles he invokes are treated either as things known to
exist or as candidates-for-reality, things which might exist and do
exist if Berkeley&rsquo;s corpuscular speculations are on the mark.
Berkeley&rsquo;s realism about corpuscles is perhaps most obvious
where it is implicitly contrasted with explicit instrumentalism about
forces:</p>

<blockquote>
We are not therefore seriously to suppose, with certain mechanic
philosophers, that the minute particles of bodies have real forces or
powers, by which they act on each other, to produce the various
phenomena in nature. The minute corpuscles are impelled and directed,
that is to say, moved to and from each other, according to various
rules or laws of motion. (S 235)
</blockquote>

<p>
Berkeley never gives any parallel cautions in the case of the
corpuscles themselves. Clearly, in Berkeley&rsquo;s view, positing
corpuscles is legitimate, whereas positing forces is absurd.</p>

<p>
What justifies these radically different treatments? While
Berkeley&rsquo;s seeming confidence in much of his corpuscular
theorizing might be difficult to fully justify, there are certainly
Berkeleyian reasons to treat forces and microscopic particles
differently. For particles, even submicroscopic ones are imaginable,
by Berkeley&rsquo;s standards, which is to say that terms for them
have the ability to refer and needn&rsquo;t be regarded as
mathematical hypotheses. Furthermore, in <em>Siris</em>, unlike in
<em>De Motu</em>, Berkeley expresses (indeed, proclaims) his
idealism:</p>

<blockquote>
&hellip;for all phenomena are, to speak truly, appearances in the soul
or mind; and it hath never been explained, nor can it be explained,
how external bodies, &#64257;gures, and motions, should produce an
appearance in the mind. Those [mechanical] principles, therefore, do
not solve, if by solving is meant assigning the real, either
ef&#64257;cient or &#64257;nal, cause of appearances, but only reduce
them to general rules. (S 251)
</blockquote>

<p>
He is thus in a position to back up his view that all physical things,
since ideal, are passive and cannot possess genuine force.</p>
